From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: colour manage direct scanout via the DPU's CTM

Night mode, Peak Saturation, the SDR gain and the internal-display brightness
scale all stopped reaching the panel the moment a frame took direct scanout.

WHY IT BROKE. gamescope does colour management in its Vulkan composite and
writes 0 to CTM, GAMMA_LUT and DEGAMMA_LUT on every path -- those writes exist
to CLEAR someone else's colour management, not to use ours. That was safe only
because we always composited: the sole colour-relevant gate in the composite
decision is

    bNeedsFullComposite |= g_uOutputRotation != 0; // can't rotate planes at scanout

and our portrait panels always tripped it. DPU inline rotation removed that
trip -- upstream sets g_uOutputRotation = 0 once the scanout plane can do the
panel's orientation -- and nothing else in that block keys off the colour state.
g_bColorSliderInUse is true only while a slider is being DRAGGED, and
drm_prepare() does not fail when colour management is wanted but unsupported;
it sets output_tf = DEFAULT and carries on. So the transform was silently
dropped, with no fallback and no log line.

CONFIRMED ON HARDWARE before writing any of this, on Pocket FIT (1080-wide,
under the DPU rotator's 1088-line cap, so genuinely on the scanout path):
GAMESCOPE_COLOR_NIGHT_MODE forced to maximum via the atom is completely
invisible, and the amber appears the instant GAMESCOPE_COMPOSITE_FORCE=1 is
set. That toggle is also the regression test.

WHAT THIS DOES. Forcing a composite whenever the transform is non-identity was
rejected: it hands back exactly the GPU load inline rotation was taken to
remove (measured at 21% of the GPU and 656 mW in a game). Instead the transform
is handed to the display controller, which already has the hardware for it --
every SoC we ship has 4 DSPPs carrying both PCC and GC, so the kernel already
exposes CTM and a 1024-entry GAMMA_LUT on the CRTC.

THE CATCH, and it decides the whole shape of the patch: the DPU exposes NO
degamma block (dpu_crtc.c calls drm_crtc_enable_color_mgmt() with a degamma
size of 0), so its matrix multiplies values that are still in the panel's
encoding. gamescope's pipeline works in linear light, and a general 3x3 does
not commute with a non-linear encoding. A DIAGONAL does, exactly, because
Gamma 2.2 is a pure power law:

    ( a * e^2.2 )^(1/2.2)  ==  a^(1/2.2) * e

So a per-channel multiply in linear light is a per-channel multiply in the
encoding with the exponent moved, and nothing is approximated. That covers
night mode (an HSV multiply, hence diagonal), the SDR input gain and the
brightness scale -- the controls users actually reach for. Gamut mapping,
tonemapping and the 3D look LUT are not diagonal and keep compositing.

calcScanoutLinearScale() decides which case we are in. It deliberately takes
the SAME argument list as calcColorTransform() and sits next to it, because the
two have to agree about what the composite does and that is the only way to
keep them agreeing as the pipeline changes. When it returns nullopt the frame
composites, so the failure mode is "correct but costs GPU", never "wrong
colours".

IT DOES NOT REFUSE OVER THE GAMUT MAPPING, and that is a deliberate reversal of
how this patch first worked. Refusing whenever the primaries matrix was
non-identity sounds right and was measured WRONG on Pocket FIT: at gamescope's
default sdrGamutWideness of 0.5 the SDR source colorimetry is generic-wide-gamut
while the panel is not, so the matrix is never identity and EVERY frame
composited -- one plane committed instead of two, with night mode merely off.
That surrenders the whole thing inline rotation was taken to win, to preserve
something that was never there: direct scanout NEVER applied the gamut mapping.
Before any of this it wrote 0 to CTM, GAMMA_LUT and DEGAMMA_LUT and ran no
Vulkan pass, i.e. it passed the source colorimetry straight through. So the
question this function answers is not "is the composite a pure scale?" but
"given that scanout passes the source through unchanged, as it always has, what
scale does it still need?"

The honest consequence: a scanned-out frame and a composited one differ by the
gamut mapping. That difference is not new -- it was there for every frame the
old code scanned out -- but it can now appear and disappear as frames switch
paths. Operator-checked on Pocket FIT with night mode off: not visible. Carrying
it in hardware is not an option anyway, since a real primaries matrix has
negative off-diagonal terms and msm's CONVERT_S3_15() discards the sign bit.

drm_supports_crtc_ctm() is a SEPARATE capability from drm_supports_color_mgmt().
The latter tests AMD's per-PLANE properties and selects a whole pipeline
(output_tf, blend_tf, per-plane shaper and 3D LUT blobs); it is hard-false on
msm and must stay that way, since flipping it true would select the AMD plane
path rather than enable this one. Nothing changes on AMD: there
drm_scanout_is_color_managed() returns true immediately and this code is inert.

WHY THE CTM IS SET EVEN WHEN IT IS THE IDENTITY. msm only reserves DSPPs for a
CRTC whose state carries a CTM or a GAMMA_LUT, and only during a MODESET. If we
left it null while the transform happened to be identity, the first touch of
the night mode slider would need a modeset mid-flip -- and that commit would be
REJECTED, because the kernel no longer forces allow_modeset on userspace's
behalf (that hack is removed by the "only reassign resources when the encoder
is reprogrammed" patch in our kernel's 0515, which this depends on). Keeping a
CTM for the life of the mode means the DSPPs are already in the topology and a
new matrix is an ordinary property write on an ordinary non-blocking flip.

THIS PATCH REQUIRES THAT KERNEL PATCH. Without it, the first CTM makes
dpu_crtc_atomic_check() re-reserve resources without the encoder being
reprogrammed, cstate->mixers[].lm_ctl and phys->hw_ctl diverge, CTL_FLUSH is
written with zero, and the display freezes with the pipe still fetching.

THE INVARIANT THAT KEEPS IT HONEST: the CTM is non-identity ONLY for a frame
nothing composited. The composite applies the same transform, so a CTM left
standing across a composited frame would apply it TWICE -- night mode would
visibly double-darken the moment the Steam overlay came up. drm_prepare() is
reached from three places and each one now states which kind of frame it is
submitting.

That also rules out PARTIAL composition while the DPU carries a transform:
partial composition scans out the base layer and composites the overlays onto a
second one, and a CRTC matrix sits after blending, so it would catch the
composited half twice. It is refused in two places on purpose -- clearing
bWantsPartialComposite only stops us CHOOSING it, while a failed scanout
attempt would otherwise fall into building one anyway.

GAMMA_LUT is left cleared. A per-channel curve could carry a real transfer
function, but nothing we ship needs one yet and the diagonal already lands
exactly; that block is where it would go.

Only the sign-magnitude packing and the encoded-scale conversion live in
DRMColorPipeline.h, so both are unit tested without a display.

RE-ROLLED 2026-09-14: regenerated against a base without the 0008-0013 EDID/HDR series,
which was retired when HDR was dropped (issue #93). Nothing here changed in intent -- the only
difference is that hunks which used to carry HDR-introduced context (create_color_mgmt_luts's
bTraditionalHDROutput parameter, the DrmHdrOutputRequiresVulkanComposition call site, and test-list
entries for deleted tests) now sit on the pristine signatures instead. Generated with git diff, not
hand-edited.
